{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n2le03ob10re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n2le03ob10re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n2le03ob10re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Sam</strong>: Catherine, I can't understand the term \u201cscripting language\u201d. Can you talk to me about it?<br/>\n<strong>Catherine</strong>: Yes, scripting languages contain commands presented in a computer in order to carry out specific functions and provide scripts that can work on the server-side and client-side at multiple levels. As a result users can control icons, keystrokes and some other different tools as any other program features always available to perform innumerable tasks. Thus, scripting languages are in fact, programming languages.", "time": "0"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n2le03ob10re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Sam</strong>: I understand, but continue, I must know what a script is. How do I find scripting languages in context?<br/>\n<strong>Catherine</strong>: A script comprises a number of text lines in a file, when a user appeals to a file, the script language processor parses each line; it means, an application programmer writes program codes in form of scripts that can be interpreted and executed one at a time. An example is: a system administrator who wants to keep record of the \u2018log in' times of users. He can decide to use a scripting language to facilitate the automating routine task using the multitasking and multiuser utilities it has. For such purposes, you can work with JavaScript, VBScript, AppleScript, Python, CoffeeScript and some other scripting languages.", "time": "36000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n2le03ob10re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Sam</strong>: I know that shell programming is the most common use of scripting languages.<br/>\n<strong>Catherine</strong>: I think you can exchange that information with me, go ahead!<br/>\n<strong>Sam</strong>: Well, shell scripting controls automating routine tasks, especially, in multiusers systems running with UNIX. Time must bring changes in programming methods and that's why programmers look for languages that combine interactive and fast nature of shells. These are structural and convenient features that can work for an effective pattern-matching, data analysis and some other tasks. This explains, the emergence of popular scripting languages such as the ones you mentioned before.", "time": "92000"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about scripting languages", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can obtain information about scripting languages in different contexts.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can exchange information about scripting languages.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "How can we deal with repetitive tasks when executing a program?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Do you consider necessary to have a programming code to automate common tasks? Why? Why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Introducing Scripting Languages", "type": "free_type_text_area"}]}, {"id": 1, "type": "STI", "questions": [{"id": 0, "responses": [{"selected": 0, "